area: tabledoc
case: 17-csv-trailing-delimiter
verification: explicit-divergence
oracle: python
projection: tabledoc
reason: lib/fetch/tabledoc.ml's reader loses a field that is empty and last in its record: the delimiter switches to Start_field without saving, and the newline's save only happens when a byte had been read, so the trailing empty cell never reaches the header and the width follows it. Python's csv yields the empty trailing cell, so the oracle renders a third column named column3 that the port does not have. The row is padded to the header's width either way, so a file whose every line ends with a delimiter loses its last column silently rather than failing — which is why this is pinned and raised instead of normalised away.
--- oracle
delimiter= ,
table= true
render= $  object(1)\n$.rows  array(1)\n$.rows[0]  object(3)\n$.rows[0].a  \"1\"\n$.rows[0].b  \"2\"\n$.rows[0].column3  \"\"
--- port
delimiter= ,
table= true
render= $  object(1)\n$.rows  array(1)\n$.rows[0]  object(2)\n$.rows[0].a  \"1\"\n$.rows[0].b  \"2\"
